0:{"buildId":"JFSdapXluxcZ7WLb1GQKv","data":[{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen flex-col bg-background text-foreground","children":[["$","$L2",null,{}],["$","main",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":["$","article",null,{"className":"py-12 sm:py-16","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center border-[3px] border-border px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs font-bold uppercase bg-info text-info-foreground","children":"Critical reasoning"}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-5 text-4xl font-black leading-tight sm:text-5xl","children":"A Guide to Common Fallacies in Today's Media"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-ink-soft","children":"Five reasoning traps appear again and again in social feeds. Learn how to recognize them before they shape what you believe or share."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-mono text-xs font-bold","children":["$","$L3",null,{"messageKey":"article.minuteRead","replacements":{"minutes":7}}]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto mt-10 max-w-3xl border-t-[3px] border-border px-4 pt-9 sm:px-6","children":[[["$","section","Why fallacies thrive online",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"Why fallacies thrive online"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"Logical fallacies are errors in reasoning that make an argument unsound, even when its conclusion happens to be true. Social posts reward speed, brevity, and certainty, so oversimplified arguments can travel farther than careful explanations. Learning their patterns gives you a practical pause button before you react or share."}]]}],["$","section","1. Attacking the messenger (Ad Hominem)",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"1. Attacking the messenger (Ad Hominem)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"An ad hominem attack targets the person making a claim instead of testing the claim itself. Someone might dismiss a sound proposal because its presenter made a mistake years ago. A person's history may affect credibility, but it does not settle whether the present evidence is valid. Ask: what part of the argument was actually answered?"}]]}],["$","section","2. The distortion tactic (Straw Man)",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"2. The distortion tactic (Straw Man)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"A straw man replaces someone's real position with a weaker or more extreme version that is easier to attack. A call for sensible restrictions, for example, may be recast as a demand for a total ban. Compare the response with the original words: if they do not match, the real argument is still standing."}]]}],["$","section","3. Confusing coincidence with cause",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"3. Confusing coincidence with cause"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"When two events happen together, it is tempting to assume that one caused the other. This is the post hoc fallacy: after this, therefore because of this. Correlation can be a useful clue, but another factor may explain both events. Look for a plausible mechanism, competing explanations, and evidence that rules them out."}]]}],"$L4","$L5","$L6","$L7"],"$L8"]}]]}]}],"$L9"]}],["$La","$Lb"],"$Lc"]}],"isPartial":"$@d","staleTime":"$e","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$Lf","isPartial":"$@10","staleTime":"$e","varyParams":null},{"rsc":"$L11","isPartial":"$@12","staleTime":"$e","varyParams":"$13"},{"rsc":"$L14","isPartial":"$@15","staleTime":"$e","varyParams":"$13"}],"isUpgradeableISRFallback":false,"a":"$@16","rootVaryParams":null,"needsRuntimeRequest":"$@17"}
