4:["$","section","4. The all-or-nothing trap (False Dilemma)",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"4. The all-or-nothing trap (False Dilemma)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"A false dilemma presents only two options when more choices exist. The unwanted option is then made to look impossible or dangerous, pushing you toward the speaker's preferred answer. Before accepting the choice, ask what middle ground, alternative, or combination has been left out."}]]}]
5:["$","section","5. The chain reaction (Slippery Slope)",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"5. The chain reaction (Slippery Slope)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"A slippery-slope argument claims that one small step will inevitably trigger a chain of increasingly extreme consequences. Sometimes risks really do compound, but each link needs evidence. Ask where the chain could stop, what safeguards exist, and whether the predicted outcome has actually followed in comparable cases."}]]}]
6:["$","section","How to level up your thinking",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"How to level up your thinking"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 text-base leading-8 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":"Critical thinking is less about instantly finding the right answer than examining how well an answer is supported. When a post makes you react, pause and restate its claim in neutral language. Then ask: what is the evidence, does it support this exact claim, what alternatives were ignored, and what would change my mind?"}]]}]
7:["$","section","References",{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl font-black leading-tight text-info","children":"References"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-ink-soft sm:text-lg","children":[["$","li","Zarvana. \"8 Fallacies You're Likely Falling For on Social Media.\"",{"children":"Zarvana. \"8 Fallacies You're Likely Falling For on Social Media.\""}],["$","li","Western Technical College. \"Fallacies: Illogical Arguments - Fake News and Fake Facts.\"",{"children":"Western Technical College. \"Fallacies: Illogical Arguments - Fake News and Fake Facts.\""}],["$","li","Wikipedia. \"List of fallacies.\"",{"children":"Wikipedia. \"List of fallacies.\""}],["$","li","University of Iowa. \"Logical Fallacies - Evaluating Online Information.\"",{"children":"University of Iowa. \"Logical Fallacies - Evaluating Online Information.\""}],["$","li","Owen M. Williamson, University of Texas at El Paso. \"Master List of Logical Fallacies.\"",{"children":"Owen M. Williamson, University of Texas at El Paso. \"Master List of Logical Fallacies.\""}]]}]]}]
